{
  "_title": "Vulcain 2-like hydrolox: dump-cooled extension and turbine exhaust film",
  "_comment": "A 1.2 MN LOX/LH2 first-stage engine at 11.5 MPa, MR 6.1 and area ratio 58, cooled in the arrangement of the Vulcain 2 nozzle: a regenerative chamber jacket to an area ratio of four, an extension circuit that dump-cools the next stretch of the nozzle with hydrogen and lays that hydrogen as a film, and the gas generator's turbine exhaust injected as a second film over the radiation-cooled shell beyond. The chamber jacket is a single counterflow pass (architecture A) of 180 circular GRCop-42 channels carrying 30 kg/s of hydrogen from 15 MPa and 40 K. It spends 5.96 MPa and peaks at 601 K against an 800 K limit. The extension circuit (architecture D) runs coflow from the joint at an area ratio of four to twelve in 400 channels: 3 kg/s of hydrogen from 10 MPa and 40 K, which leaves at 311 K through a 30 mm slot. A fuel-rich gas generator at a mixture ratio of 0.9 and 10 MPa drives a turbine at a pressure ratio of 15 and an efficiency of 0.6, and its 10 kg/s of exhaust enters at 608 K through a 60 mm slot at an area ratio of 13. The two films stack by superposition over the C103 shell, which peaks at 865 K, 808 K inside its limit, against a recovery temperature of 3214 to 3348 K. Both slots run at 0.06 to 0.11 of the core velocity, far outside the 0.9 to 1.15 band SP-8124's entrainment closure was written for, and the run reports Hatch-Papell beside it. Counting the dumped hydrogen and the exhaust as flow beside the chamber and their thrust kept apart from the core, the engine delivers 429.3 s at its design back pressure (432.3 s if the films mix fully). The flows and dimensions are chosen to show the architecture; they are not the Vulcain 2's. Where D is viable: first stages and long bells where the throat must see cold coolant. Heritage: the Vulcain 2 nozzle extension, dump-cooled into a film with turbine exhaust injected below it.",
